# jvn environment — copy to `.env` in this checkout and fill in what you need.
# Lookup order: real environment wins, then this checkout's `.env`, then ~/.config/jvn/env.
# The `.env` is read only from the jev-navigator checkout, never from a repository under analysis,
# and only jvn's own settings (TYPESAFE_*, JEV_NAVIGATOR_*, SYSTEM_ONE_*, DREX_*) are taken from
# it. An installed jvn run outside a checkout uses the real environment and ~/.config/jvn/env.

# --- Single decision service (simple setup) ---------------------------------
# The TypeSafe Jev endpoint (default) or any wire-compatible System-One service:
#TYPESAFE_API_KEY=tsk-...
#TYPESAFE_BASE_URL=https://api.typesafe.ai
#TYPESAFE_DEFAULT_MODEL=jev-latest

# Drex by Nace.AI — the drex route (pointing TYPESAFE_BASE_URL at Drex does not work: Drex
# refuses the Jev client's checks):
#SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES=drex
#DREX_API_KEY=nace_sk_...

# --- Route table: named routes with automatic fallback ----------------------
# The first route is primary; when a call fails, the next is asked. A registered route
# (jev, drex: see src/jev_navigator/adapters/registry.py) brings its endpoint, model and key
# variable, and never uses another service's key:
#SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES=drex,jev
# Per-route settings override the adapter's (the drex endpoint stays https://drex.nace.ai):
#SYSTEM_ONE_DREX_MODEL=drex-latest
#SYSTEM_ONE_DREX_API_KEY=nace_sk_...
#SYSTEM_ONE_JEV_API_KEY=tsk-...
# Your own model server is just another route: any http:// or https:// endpoint speaking
# POST /v1/systemone (no query string or credentials in the URL; a bad one fails at startup).
# The key is optional (no key, no Authorization header). Name each checkpoint in MODEL and in
# the served "model" field, never a moving alias: stored answers are reused by that name.
#SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES=decider,jev
#SYSTEM_ONE_DECIDER_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:8900
#SYSTEM_ONE_DECIDER_MODEL=decider-4b-v1
#SYSTEM_ONE_DECIDER_API_KEY=local-dev
# Every route may set its own timeout (seconds per attempt) and retry count, for a server that
# is slower or flakier than a hosted API:
#SYSTEM_ONE_DECIDER_TIMEOUT=120
#SYSTEM_ONE_DECIDER_RETRIES=0
# An adapter that lives outside this library, such as a model that runs in this process
# (a LocalModelClient subclass), is named as module:Class:
#SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES=classifier,drex
#SYSTEM_ONE_CLASSIFIER_ADAPTER=my_models.classifier:ClassifierClient

# --- Per question type: a route table for Check, Pick or Rate questions -----
# A type's table replaces SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES for that type, so list its fallbacks too. A type
# without a table uses SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES (or Jev, when that is unset). A request mixing types
# bound for different tables is split into one call per table and counts once against the
# call budget. Here your classifier answers yes/no checks, falling back to Drex, and Drex
# answers the picks and rates:
#SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES_CHECK=classifier,drex
#SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES=drex
# SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES_PICK and SYSTEM_ONE_ROUTES_RATE work the same way.

# --- Per route: bars for a model that runs hotter or colder -----------------
# The JEV_NAVIGATOR_* thresholds (yes at 0.80, no at 0.20, a confident pick at 0.70) are the
# shared scale. A route whose model needs other bars sets its own, and its answers are mapped
# onto the shared scale (its bars land on the shared ones), so every verdict, ranking and report
# compares like with like. The journal keeps the answers as the service sent them, and a resumed
# search must use the same bars. Drex (drex-v1.5) rated near misses 0.83 to 0.88 in live
# searches; 0.9 is a starting point, not a value measured on labelled data:
#SYSTEM_ONE_DREX_NOUL_YES_AT=0.9
# SYSTEM_ONE_<NAME>_NOUL_NO_AT and SYSTEM_ONE_<NAME>_CHOICE_MIN_CONFIDENCE work the same way.
